m in a Lutheran Church  costumes and makeup are an integral part  of the Indian dance tradition the bindi  a red dot on the forehead provides the  final touch it's placed at the center of  the forehead close to the eyebrows  putting this mark on the forehead it is  it is a kind of beautifying oneself and  also it's it's a sign of in Indian  culture red mark is a sign of purity of  mind and body and and artistically this  also looks a very very attractive point  today father saju is presenting his holy  spirit choreography a highly unusual  performance in this Lutheran Church the  Protestant spectators are fascinated by  this new approach to their faith  in a city like Hamburg we experience a  variety of cultures every day and I  personally think it's important that we  try to understand each other to talk to  each other  inter-religious dialogue is very  important to me not just to talk about  each other but to learn from each other  to hear how other people express  themselves and what they feel then I see  perhaps what unites us and also notice  the differences that it's important to  respect each other as we are back in  India this is where father Sancho is  making his dream a reality  building up the cultural center he calls  collodion which is Hindi for art of art  with his reputation as a teacher he's  already attracting dancers from all over  the country  the profit from these dance courses and  performances is all invested in his  ambitious project this is a new building  that we built recently this is a  multipurpose building mostly for  conducting dance theater music workshops  or seminars so now we are conducting a  workshop a seminar on Indian classical  dance that is a Bharatanatyam  running the dance center requires a lot  of time and energy and organizing travel  arrangements for the dance groups also  takes a lot of effort the students want  expert teachers and certificates in  future father Saji would like to  cooperate with a university but for that  more guest housing would be needed his  former mentor thinks the 49 year old  demands too much of himself I don't know  how much the time he gets with such  responsibility of building up a life an  institution which is his passion which  is his doing work I think what he needs  is somebody will help him all through  very confident a confident you know move  in with whom he can share the work so  that he has enough time for his dance  also on his trips abroad father Sanju  makes lots of useful contacts his fellow  Jesuits in Switzerland for instance  bought him this nine seater Jeep  the keys are handed over in Kolkata and  in good Catholic tradition the drivers  and the vehicle are blessed with holy  water we have a little more easier and  smooth travel to the city and also for  the work in two different places that we  take our groups whether they travel for  business of nature that them always find  Christ to the companions of the journey  who lives and reigns forever and ever  on its maiden voyage through the narrow  streets the bright red Jeep sails over  the potholes there's a second blessing  awaiting it in the village morning  prayer at the cultural center the next  day Julian Lutz is from Switzerland and  working as a volunteer at a dance center  he says he's attracted by the way  spirituality is lived in India it's a  completely different world and I see a  lot more tolerance here than I ever  imagined people of all religions are  welcome at father Sanju's cultural  center that is evident in the chapel  he's building it will be dedicated to  Mary the mother of Jesus for Hindus  mother mother cult or mother with child  is very attractive mother figure so  Hindus also come and pray they don't  think that its mother  Mary it's the Christians goddess and  even for Muslims mother Mary is part of  the qur'anic no experience  father saju turns to his fellow Jesuits  in Kolkata for support he's encouraged  by their enthusiasm for the direction in  which he plans to develop his center  he's also making this as a forum for  dialogue a dialogue between cultures  between religions dialogue between  people he's able to bring no European  brother into his Center listen that you  say quite you know attuned with the  today's globalized world the Jesuit  priest is also attuned to the needs of  the poor villagers these isolated  communities are only accessible via  narrow mud tracks there's heavy rainfall  in these parts and the roads are often  impassable over that mark they put  bricks now they slowly start using  concrete so therefore a slow gradual  development of the passage system  most of the villagers here are done it  traditionally regarded as untouchable in  the Indian caste system many of them  have no educational opportunities  the Jesuits help them as much as they  can we not only do the spiritual help  you know the spiritual nourishment but  also we give educational help if we have  a school here more than 150 years old  the poor people here not only lack  education often they don't even have  adequate housing father's a Jew buys  them basic building materials not enough  to meet all their needs but at least to  make a start so we cannot do a finished  furnished house so it's only the the the  basic support they need so they get a  hope then it they need a courage to  start off now that's what most people  need later slowly slowly they add their  own they also work and they somehow you  know build  finish you may take say two years three  years five years that doesn't matter but  they can live they have a roof over  their head  the priest is only able to help a few  families in this way his funds are very  limited  from time to time father Sanju was also  able to provide work for the villagers  they cook in a kitchen or work in the  gardens there's very high unemployment  in this region inevitably his  flourishing cultural project in the  middle of this destitute part of West  Bengal also provokes resentment I have  sometimes little problems will be with  the local local clubs for example I that  is not anything connected with the  religion that is they want some money so  they block sometimes my work here they  simply want you know to have a control  over what we do here  the brand-new vehicle also gives rise to  envy lots of people here have to make do  with a bicycle fighting their way  through the traffic  father sanju already has a motorcycle  license and is familiar with the  problems  learning to drive a car presents new  challenges to drive the car in Calcutta  it is very difficult you have to be  there to be really expert driver to do a  good job in Calcutta because a very kind  the streets are congested and now the  vehicles are increasing day by day and  the all kinds of vehicles are on the  road and there are no you know different  lanes for for recycles or bicycles and  tricycles  after his driving lesson father saju has  to rush off this evening is the opening  of his dance festival in Kolkata he  hopes it will raise awareness of his  cultural center Kolkata has four and a  half million residents and is known as  India's cultural capital Christian  Catholic newspaper about the program  jesuit center to organize dance festival  and seminar with our photograph  fatherís Hodja buys candles to welcome  the guests of honor  the festival is taking place at st.  Xavier's College a Jesuit school that  has served Calcutta for more than 150  years after some delay the lighting  technicians arrived they were held up in  one of the city's legendary  traffic jams the guests will be arriving  in just a few hours  there's no charge to attend father saju  is expecting an audience of about 500 a  number of them are important dance  teachers gurus especially for classical  Indian dance and music and arts the the  teacher-student relationship is very  important it's it's that we call it a  guru coolest system or guru shishya  system so we are like their children  they are like our parents they are like  our preceptors or we emulate them no  they are the people who we look up to  for the learning of  arts as well as for our life some of  them are they are our philosophers and  guides the dancers know what's at stake  it's the future of father saju Center  just 20 kilometers from here this  evening they're performing for  Calcutta's cultural elite  from the dressing-room father saju gives  final instructions but before the show  starts the volunteer from Switzerland  also has to dress for the occasion  then the audience starts to arrive the  visiting dance teachers and gurus open  the festival brother sajju and various  dance groups present their work  the highlight of the evening is a  performance by father seduced own  teacher professor Chandra Sekhar a  highly respected guru who is now 80  years old the evening is a triumph for  father saju and his dance center it took  place with the grace of God as we wanted  to take place and I feel so peaceful and  so happy and excited within souvenirs  snapshots of this important evening the  next day the dancers meet for lunch at  father Sanju's but the priest himself  doesn't have any time he's already  planning his next tour he needs to  promote his Khalid ayah centre abroad to  raise money for its development it needs  many more people many more generous  hearts to make this heart you know heart  of art really vibrate with the art  father saju is a tireless dancer and  priest in ten years he says his Center  will be part of a university and he  himself will never stop dancing the  Bible
 

